    "title": "Why You Wake Up at 3 AM and How to Fall Back Asleep",
    "description": "Learn why middle-of-the-night waking happens, how your sleep architecture changes by 3 AM, and when to discuss sleep disruption with a doctor."
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "question": "Why do I wake up at the exact same time every night?",
      "answer": "Your sleep cycles run on consistent 90 to 110 minute loops, and your circadian rhythm operates on a predictable schedule. If you go to bed at a regular hour, your transition into lighter REM-dominant sleep will naturally happen at roughly the same clock time each night."
    },
    {
      "question": "Should I look at the clock when I wake up in the middle of the night?",
      "answer": "It is best to keep clock faces turned away. Looking at the time activates calculation and worry about how much rest you have left, which raises your heart rate and increases mental alertness."
    },
    {
      "question": "Is waking up at 3 AM a sign of a hormone imbalance?",
      "answer": "Not usually. Cortisol naturally begins a slow, healthy rise in the early morning hours in all healthy adults to prepare the body for waking. When baseline stress or light sleep stages coincide with this rhythm, you are simply more likely to notice the awakening."
    }
  ]
